{"id": "code-review-001", "clusterId": "code-review", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Review the following code. Identify bugs and explain their impact; suggest fixes. Be concise and specific.\n\nPython — computes the average of a list of numbers:\n\ndef average(nums):\n    total = 0\n    for n in nums:\n        total += n\n    return total / (len(nums) + 1)"}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the code review's ability to find the seeded defect(s). Seeded bug: divides by len(nums) + 1 instead of len(nums), so every average is systematically too small. 10 = identifies the bug precisely, explains its concrete impact, and proposes a correct fix. 7-9 = finds the bug but impact or fix is vague/slightly wrong. 4-6 = notices something is wrong in the right area without diagnosing the actual bug. 1-3 = only style/nitpick comments, misses the bug. 0 = declares the code correct or is off-topic. ", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "code-review-002", "clusterId": "code-review", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Review the following code. Identify bugs and explain their impact; suggest fixes. Be concise and specific.\n\nJavaScript — debounced click handler factory:\n\nfunction makeHandlers(buttons) {\n  const handlers = [];\n  for (var i = 0; i < buttons.length; i++) {\n    handlers.push(() => console.log(\"clicked\", buttons[i].id));\n  }\n  return handlers;\n}"}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the code review's ability to find the seeded defect(s). Seeded bug: the loop uses `var`, so every closure captures the same binding; after the loop i === buttons.length, and every handler logs buttons[buttons.length] which is undefined (TypeError reading .id). Fix: use `let` or capture i in an IIFE/parameter. 10 = identifies the bug precisely, explains its concrete impact, and proposes a correct fix. 7-9 = finds the bug but impact or fix is vague/slightly wrong. 4-6 = notices something is wrong in the right area without diagnosing the actual bug. 1-3 = only style/nitpick comments, misses the bug. 0 = declares the code correct or is off-topic. ", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "code-review-003", "clusterId": "code-review", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Review the following code. Identify bugs and explain their impact; suggest fixes. Be concise and specific.\n\nPython — appends an audit entry to a shared log list:\n\ndef log_event(event, log=[]):\n    log.append(event)\n    return log"}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the code review's ability to find the seeded defect(s). Seeded bug: mutable default argument. The default list is created once at function definition and shared across all calls that omit `log`, so events leak between unrelated calls. Fix: default to None and create a new list inside. 10 = identifies the bug precisely, explains its concrete impact, and proposes a correct fix. 7-9 = finds the bug but impact or fix is vague/slightly wrong. 4-6 = notices something is wrong in the right area without diagnosing the actual bug. 1-3 = only style/nitpick comments, misses the bug. 0 = declares the code correct or is off-topic. ", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "code-review-004", "clusterId": "code-review", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Review the following code. Identify bugs and explain their impact; suggest fixes. Be concise and specific.\n\nPython + SQL — looks up a user record:\n\ndef find_user(conn, username):\n    query = \"SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '\" + username + \"'\"\n    cursor = conn.execute(query)\n    return cursor.fetchone()"}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the code review's ability to find the seeded defect(s). Seeded bug: SQL injection via string concatenation of an unsanitized username into the query. An input like ' OR '1'='1 returns all users; a hostile input can drop tables. Fix: parameterized query / bound parameters. 10 = identifies the bug precisely, explains its concrete impact, and proposes a correct fix. 7-9 = finds the bug but impact or fix is vague/slightly wrong. 4-6 = notices something is wrong in the right area without diagnosing the actual bug. 1-3 = only style/nitpick comments, misses the bug. 0 = declares the code correct or is off-topic. ", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "code-review-005", "clusterId": "code-review", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Review the following code. Identify bugs and explain their impact; suggest fixes. Be concise and specific.\n\nJavaScript — paginates an array (page is 1-based):\n\nfunction getPage(items, page, pageSize) {\n  const start = page * pageSize;\n  return items.slice(start, start + pageSize);\n}"}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the code review's ability to find the seeded defect(s). Seeded bug: off-by-one in the offset. For 1-based pages the start index must be (page - 1) * pageSize; as written, page 1 skips the first pageSize items. 10 = identifies the bug precisely, explains its concrete impact, and proposes a correct fix. 7-9 = finds the bug but impact or fix is vague/slightly wrong. 4-6 = notices something is wrong in the right area without diagnosing the actual bug. 1-3 = only style/nitpick comments, misses the bug. 0 = declares the code correct or is off-topic. ", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "code-review-006", "clusterId": "code-review", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Review the following code. Identify bugs and explain their impact; suggest fixes. Be concise and specific.\n\nGo — counts word frequencies:\n\nfunc countWords(words []string) map[string]int {\n\tvar counts map[string]int\n\tfor _, w := range words {\n\t\tcounts[w]++\n\t}\n\treturn counts\n}"}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the code review's ability to find the seeded defect(s). Seeded bug: `counts` is a nil map (declared but never initialized); writing counts[w]++ panics at runtime with 'assignment to entry in nil map'. Fix: counts := make(map[string]int). 10 = identifies the bug precisely, explains its concrete impact, and proposes a correct fix. 7-9 = finds the bug but impact or fix is vague/slightly wrong. 4-6 = notices something is wrong in the right area without diagnosing the actual bug. 1-3 = only style/nitpick comments, misses the bug. 0 = declares the code correct or is off-topic. ", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "code-review-007", "clusterId": "code-review", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Review the following code. Identify bugs and explain their impact; suggest fixes. Be concise and specific.\n\nJava — checks whether a command-line argument equals 'yes':\n\npublic static boolean confirmed(String arg) {\n    if (arg == \"yes\") {\n        return true;\n    }\n    return false;\n}"}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the code review's ability to find the seeded defect(s). Seeded bug: compares String references with == instead of arg.equals(\"yes\") (or \"yes\".equals(arg)); two distinct String objects with the same contents compare false, so confirmed(\"yes\") can return false. Note also potential NPE direction if arg is null — 'yes'.equals(arg) is safer. 10 = identifies the bug precisely, explains its concrete impact, and proposes a correct fix. 7-9 = finds the bug but impact or fix is vague/slightly wrong. 4-6 = notices something is wrong in the right area without diagnosing the actual bug. 1-3 = only style/nitpick comments, misses the bug. 0 = declares the code correct or is off-topic. ", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "code-review-008", "clusterId": "code-review", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Review the following code. Identify bugs and explain their impact; suggest fixes. Be concise and specific.\n\nPython — builds a multiplication table from a template row:\n\ndef make_table(n):\n    row = [0] * n\n    table = [row] * n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(n):\n            table[i][j] = (i + 1) * (j + 1)\n    return table"}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the code review's ability to find the seeded defect(s). Seeded bug: aliasing/shallow copy. [row] * n puts n references to the SAME list in table, so every assignment table[i][j] mutates all rows; the final table has n identical rows equal to the last-written values. Fix: build each row independently (e.g., [0]*n inside the loop or a list comprehension). 10 = identifies the bug precisely, explains its concrete impact, and proposes a correct fix. 7-9 = finds the bug but impact or fix is vague/slightly wrong. 4-6 = notices something is wrong in the right area without diagnosing the actual bug. 1-3 = only style/nitpick comments, misses the bug. 0 = declares the code correct or is off-topic. ", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "code-review-009", "clusterId": "code-review", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Review the following code. Identify bugs and explain their impact; suggest fixes. Be concise and specific.\n\nPython — determines whether a temperature reading is freezing:\n\ndef is_freezing(temp_str):\n    temp = temp_str.strip()\n    if temp is \"0\":\n        return True\n    return False"}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the code review's ability to find the seeded defect(s). Seeded bug: uses `is` (identity) instead of == to compare strings; small-string interning makes it appear to work in tests, but a non-interned \"0\" (e.g., built at runtime) returns False. Fix: use ==. (Bonus: this also emits a SyntaxWarning on modern Python.) 10 = identifies the bug precisely, explains its concrete impact, and proposes a correct fix. 7-9 = finds the bug but impact or fix is vague/slightly wrong. 4-6 = notices something is wrong in the right area without diagnosing the actual bug. 1-3 = only style/nitpick comments, misses the bug. 0 = declares the code correct or is off-topic. ", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "code-review-010", "clusterId": "code-review", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Review the following code. Identify bugs and explain their impact; suggest fixes. Be concise and specific.\n\nPython — recursively computes factorial:\n\ndef factorial(n):\n    if n == 1:\n        return 1\n    return n * factorial(n - 1)"}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the code review's ability to find the seeded defect(s). Seeded bug: the base case only stops at n == 1, so factorial(0) never terminates — it recurses through -1, -2, ... until RecursionError. Fix: use n <= 1 (or n == 0) as the base case. 10 = identifies the bug precisely, explains its concrete impact, and proposes a correct fix. 7-9 = finds the bug but impact or fix is vague/slightly wrong. 4-6 = notices something is wrong in the right area without diagnosing the actual bug. 1-3 = only style/nitpick comments, misses the bug. 0 = declares the code correct or is off-topic. Deduct if the review claims factorial(5) is wrong — it returns 120 correctly; the defect is specific to inputs below 1.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "code-review-011", "clusterId": "code-review", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Review the following code. Identify bugs and explain their impact; suggest fixes. Be concise and specific.\n\nTypeScript — returns a sorted copy of leaderboard entries:\n\ninterface Entry { name: string; score: number; }\n\nfunction topThree(entries: Entry[]): Entry[] {\n  const sorted = entries.sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score);\n  return sorted.slice(0, 3);\n}"}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the code review's ability to find the seeded defect(s). Seeded bug: Array.prototype.sort mutates the array in place, so the caller's `entries` list is unexpectedly reordered despite the function name implying a copy. Fix: [...entries].sort(...) or entries.slice().sort(...). 10 = identifies the bug precisely, explains its concrete impact, and proposes a correct fix. 7-9 = finds the bug but impact or fix is vague/slightly wrong. 4-6 = notices something is wrong in the right area without diagnosing the actual bug. 1-3 = only style/nitpick comments, misses the bug. 0 = declares the code correct or is off-topic. ", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "code-review-012", "clusterId": "code-review", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Review the following code. Identify bugs and explain their impact; suggest fixes. Be concise and specific.\n\nPython — reads a JSON config file and returns the settings dict:\n\nimport json\n\ndef load_settings(path):\n    f = open(path)\n    data = json.load(f)\n    return data"}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the code review's ability to find the seeded defect(s). Seeded bug: the file handle is never closed — a resource leak that exhausts file descriptors under repeated calls (and flushes/locking issues on some platforms). Fix: use `with open(path) as f:`. 10 = identifies the bug precisely, explains its concrete impact, and proposes a correct fix. 7-9 = finds the bug but impact or fix is vague/slightly wrong. 4-6 = notices something is wrong in the right area without diagnosing the actual bug. 1-3 = only style/nitpick comments, misses the bug. 0 = declares the code correct or is off-topic. ", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "code-review-013", "clusterId": "code-review", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Review the following code. Identify bugs and explain their impact; suggest fixes. Be concise and specific.\n\nSQL — removes expired sessions from the sessions table:\n\nDELETE FROM sessions\nWHERE user_id IN (SELECT id FROM users);"}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the code review's ability to find the seeded defect(s). Seeded bug: the intended filter on expiry is missing — the statement deletes sessions for EVERY user in the users table, not just expired ones, effectively wiping all active sessions. Fix: add the expiry predicate (e.g., WHERE expires_at < NOW()). Also acceptable to flag that the subquery is redundant for the stated goal. 10 = identifies the bug precisely, explains its concrete impact, and proposes a correct fix. 7-9 = finds the bug but impact or fix is vague/slightly wrong. 4-6 = notices something is wrong in the right area without diagnosing the actual bug. 1-3 = only style/nitpick comments, misses the bug. 0 = declares the code correct or is off-topic. ", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "code-review-014", "clusterId": "code-review", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "Review the following code. Identify bugs and explain their impact; suggest fixes. Be concise and specific.\n\nPython — checks if a user may view a document:\n\ndef can_view(user, doc):\n    if user.is_admin or user.id == doc.owner_id and doc.is_archived:\n        return True\n    return False"}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the code review's ability to find the seeded defect(s). Seeded bug: operator precedence — `and` binds tighter than `or`, so the condition parses as user.is_admin OR (user.id == doc.owner_id AND doc.is_archived). The owner can view only archived docs and non-owner non-admins can never view; the intended rule (admin, or owner of a non-archived doc) is inverted for owners. Fix: user.is_admin or (user.id == doc.owner_id and not doc.is_archived). 10 = identifies the bug precisely, explains its concrete impact, and proposes a correct fix. 7-9 = finds the bug but impact or fix is vague/slightly wrong. 4-6 = notices something is wrong in the right area without diagnosing the actual bug. 1-3 = only style/nitpick comments, misses the bug. 0 = declares the code correct or is off-topic. ", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
